{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310778,"nonce":"oLeLEkNKyJ/VqgdMCVNJG6k4ugI=","title":"MEASURED the decode split — the attention scan is 6-7% of Mistral-7B decode at chat lengths, NOT the ~52% derived figure; the FFN is the ...","message":"Landed `5243a654`: `NUE_PROFILE_ATTN=1` splits `GQAttention.forward` into the\npart that grows with cache length (score/mask/softmax/context, or the fused\nflash kernel) and the part that does not (QKV + rope + append + oProj), across\nall five exit paths. Dumped by `GQAttention.dumpAttnProfile()` (no-arg public\nstatic, same registration constraint as `Q4Matmul.dumpPlan`).\n\n**Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 Q4_K_M, NUC (x86 i5-1250P):**\n\n| run | tok/s | scan/tok | proj/tok | scan % decode | attn % decode |\n|-----|-------|----------|----------|---------------|---------------|\n| 600 tok, avg ctx ~330 | 7.01 | 7.6 ms | 29.8 ms | **5.6%** | 27.4% |\n| 831 tok, avg ctx ~450 | 7.24 | 10.0 ms | 26.3 ms | **7.4%** | 27.0% |\n\n**The attention scan is 6-7% of decode at chat lengths. ~72% is FFN + norms +\nsampling.** Mistral's FFN is 14336 wide against hidden 4096 — that is where the\nFLOPs are. Any scan lever (SWA, better flash kernel) has a ~6% ceiling here.\n\nThe \"attention is 52% of decode\" figure I had been ranking levers on was the\nscan's share **OF ATTENTION**, not of decode — and all of attention is only\n~27%. Off by ~4x. Qwen2.5-0.5B shows the same trap: scan is 52.1% of attention\nat ctx ~475 but 18.4% of decode.\n\nScan grows ~linearly with ctx (7.6→10.0 ms for ctx 330→450) while proj is flat,\nso it only overtakes proj near ctx ~1200 and would be the largest bucket\n(~42%) at the full 4096. **So: FFN for chat lengths, scan only if 4k context is\nthe target.**\n\n- **SWA is void for this model** — the GGUF on disk is v0.2, which DROPPED\n  sliding-window attention (v0.1 had 4096). Check the model before planning it.\n- **AMX f16 cache is not the Mac's problem**: `RZT_AMX_CACHE_MB=0` gave\n  `[warm] 425.3s` vs 430.4s at the 3 GB default — identical.\n\n| | Mac (M-series, 16 GB) | NUC (8 GB, idle) |\n|---|---|---|\n| load | **438 s** | **21 s** |\n| tok/s | ~0.15 (never finished 600) | 7.0 |\n\nMac had 0.1 GB free / 5.7 GB compressed / 7.4 GB swap; NUC had 5.65 GiB\navailable. **`free -g` TRUNCATES** — the NUC reads \"6\" but is 6.82 GiB total.\nJudge a host by MemAvailable, not total. Weights are **mmap-backed** (zero-copy\nQTensors over file bytes), so they are evictable page cache, never swap: peak\nRSS 4.27 GB fits 5.65 GiB fine. On the Mac the page cache could not hold them\nso every warm pass re-read from disk. Qwen for scale: Mac Q4_K_M 63.0 tok/s,\nNUC Q6_K 45.5 tok/s.\n\nNo repo there; `git archive HEAD | gzip` is 11 MB (the worktree is 14 GB of\nuntracked artifacts). Then:\n1. **rustc 1.93 from the distro shadows rustup's 1.97** — `/usr/bin/cargo`\n   wins over `~/.cargo/bin`. The `wiggle-macro` ≥1.95 gate from the\n   cranelift/wasmtime pin (67ac8cb7) fails on the distro one. Export\n   `PATH=\"$HOME/.cargo/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"`.\n2. `LLVM_SYS_211_PREFIX=/usr/lib/llvm-21` (21.1.8 present, with static libs).\n3. **Ubuntu's llvm-21 ships no Polly static libs.** sudo needs a password, so:\n   `apt-get download libpolly-21-dev` + `dpkg-deb -x` into `~/llvm21-polly`,\n   then add `-L` — version-matched, nothing outside $HOME touched.\n4. Build `-p rayzor-tensors -p nue-plugins` too, or the run dies on a missing\n   `librayzor_tensors.so` (see [[project_three_layer_architecture]]).\n\n**TRAP that cost a rebuild:** setting `RUSTFLAGS` **OVERRIDES**\n`.cargo/config.toml`'s `target.*.rustflags`. The repo already sets\n`--export-dynamic` there; my Polly `-L` in RUSTFLAGS silently disabled it, so\nthe binary exported 0 of 485 `haxe_*` symbols and Cranelift's dlsym fallback\npanicked `can't resolve symbol haxe_channel_unbox_erased`. I nearly filed this\nas a portability bug in the repo — it was mine. Pass BOTH flags, or use\n`LIBRARY_PATH` for the `-L` so the config is not clobbered.\n\n`56c21642` — `chat.config.maxNewTokens = …` hard-failed **E0803 on Linux only**\n(receiver class unresolved → field matched by name across GenerationConfig /\nGenerationLoop / SpeculativeGenerationLoop). Annotating both bindings fixes it.\nThe compiler was RIGHT to refuse: same shape as `sb.length` in\n[[bugs_std_string_dynamic_unbox_and_erased_generics]], which still guesses.\nConfirms the x-module cluster is order-dependent — same source, different host,\ndifferent outcome.\n\nInterleaved 3 pairs/model, fresh cache + pkill per run, 600 tok, medians:\n\n| arm | Mistral-7B | Qwen-0.5B Q6_K |\n|-----|-----------|----------------|\n| pin 60154144 (x64 VNNI) | 7.037 | 41.291 |\n| pre-pin 5937fbcd | 7.028 | 41.001 |\n\n+0.13% / +0.71%, ranges fully overlap — **noise**. Mechanically expected:\n`--llvm` bulk-upgrades before main, so steady-state decode is LLVM code and\nCranelift codegen (where the VNNI lowering lives) never runs. The pin is NOT\nrefuted as a lever — it acts where CRANELIFT executes: the wasm target under\nwasmtime (relaxed-simd i8 dot → vpdpbusd) and cranelift-tier/native-fallback\nruns. Measure THERE before citing it again. Note the true pre-pin baseline was\ngit rev `5937fbcd`, not crates.io — the pin commit's \"resolved to 0.134.2\"\nstory described the failed first attempt, not the prior state.\n\nChased via gdb SIGTRAP sampling (perf is locked down: paranoid=4, no sudo;\nptrace_scope=1 so gdb must be the ANCESTOR — samplers in /tmp/sampler*.py,\ngdb disables ASLR so JIT addresses are stable ACROSS gdb runs but NEVER\ncomparable to non-gdb runs; that mismatch caused a wrong \"runs Cranelift\"\ndetour).\n\nEliminated by measurement, in order:\n- fused gate/up dispatch (NUE_FUSED_MATMUL=1): +1.7%, 3 consistent pairs — not the lever\n- never-park spin (NUE_POOL_SPINS=500000): +0.6% — null\n- serial-main Amdahl: refuted — main thread 72% INSIDE the band lambda\n- pool starvation: refuted — per-worker counters (added to SpinPool profReport:\n  busyUs/claims per participant) show ~89% utilization, all 8 uniform, decode\n  isolated by DIFFING a 1-token run (counters span load+warm too — band_ms \u003e\n  wall was that, not a bug). 258 dispatches/token.\n\n**Instruction census of the promoted band lambda (5228 instrs, disassembled\nin-session):** vpdpbusd x192 (dots — VNNI FIRES, the wired-but-dead\n`try_build_x86_vpdpbusd` works via dotI8I7), but vmulsd x280 + vcvtss2sd x32\n(branchy SOFT-f16 scale decode) + vpmulld x64, nearly all XMM-width. **~94% of\ninstructions are per-sub-block scale/f16/f64 glue around efficient dots.**\nExplains: VNNI-pin null, tree-shake-fix null, spin null — every arm was\nglue-bound. ~260 MMAC/s/thread effective.\n\nAccumulate sub-block dots in INT, multiply by the 6-bit INT scales, apply the\nf16 `d` ONCE per super-block: ~16 vmulsd/row instead of 280, 2 f16 decodes\ninstead of 32. Est. kernel 3-5x -\u003e FFN is 74% of decode -\u003e ~2-3x tok/s.\nKernel-level Haxe change in `q6DotMA4`/`subDotVec` + accumulation structure;\nmust A/B outputs (bit-exactness policy) and re-run the attn/decode splits.\n\ntree_shake resolved CallDirect/FunctionRef/MakeClosure only against the\nCURRENT module -\u003e cross-module closure refs silently dropped from the LLVM\ntier (the band lambda's 4 siblings promoted, it didn't). Fix = global\nid-\u003eowning-module index. No perf change HERE (glue-bound either tier) but any\ncross-module closure was silently losing LLVM.\n\n- `pkill -f rayzor` in an ssh one-liner kills the WRAPPER (its own cmdline\n  matches). Use `pkill -x rayzor`.\n- SpinPool profile counters span the WHOLE process (load+warm+decode):\n  isolate decode by diffing a 1-token run.\n- `nohup cmd \u0026` over ssh dies with the session; `(setsid cmd \u0026)` survives.\n\n**FIRST BANDWIDTH BENCH WAS WRONG** — a `volatile` accumulator serialised every\nload, so it measured dependent-load latency, not bandwidth. It read 40.8 GB/s\non the NUC and an absurd 22.6 GB/s on an M1 Pro. Corrected version (8\nindependent accumulators, no volatile, /tmp/bw2.c) reads:\n**NUC 41.8 GB/s (8t) / M1 Pro 104.3 GB/s (10t)**. The NUC number barely moved,\nso its conclusion survived by luck, not method. ALWAYS sanity-check a\nbandwidth number against the platform's spec sheet before building on it.\n\nDecode reads every weight once per token: 4.37 GB x 7.264 tok/s = **31.7 GB/s\n= 78% of the 40.8 GB/s achievable**. Roofline at 100% is **9.34 tok/s**.\n\n**So the whole compute-lever program was chasing 22%.** That retro-explains\nEVERY null this session — VNNI pin (+0.1%), fused dispatch (+1.7%), spin budget\n(+0.6%), tree-shake/LLVM-tier fix (0%), Q6_K int folding (+2.4%). None of them\ncould have paid: the cores are waiting on DRAM.\n\n- **Kernel micro-optimisation is DONE on this hardware.** Max remaining ~22%,\n  and only if compute overlaps memory perfectly.\n- **The only big levers move BYTES, not FLOPs:**\n  1. Smaller quant (Q4_K_M -\u003e Q4_0/IQ4 or INT8-per-row): fewer bytes/token,\n     scales tok/s ~linearly with the size cut.\n  2. Batching / speculative decode: amortise ONE weight sweep over N tokens.\n     This is where prompt-lookup or a draft model actually pays — not as a\n     compute trick but as a bandwidth divisor.\n  3. A machine with more bandwidth (the roofline is the machine, not the code).\n- **Do NOT** invest further in dot width (256-bit VNNI/SIMD32i8), fusion, or\n  pool tuning for THIS model on THIS box. Re-check the roofline first on any\n  new host — the ratio, not the tok/s, is the number that matters.\n\nq6DotMA4 did 64 f64 multiplies per super-block vs 16 vpdpbusd of dot;\nq4DotMA4 already folded scales in SIMD4i32. Same algebra applies (sc is int,\nd is common): exact in i32 (bounds in the commit), **bit-identical output** on\nQwen Q6_K, +2.4% Mistral (3/3 wins) / +1.4% Qwen Q6_K. Kept because it is\nfree accuracy + a real if small win, not because it moved the needle.\nCensus for Mistral Q4_K_M: **Q4_K 34944 calls vs Q6_K 6006** — Q6_K is 15%.\n\n| | Qwen-0.5B Q6_K (0.506 GB) | Mistral-7B Q4_K_M (4.37 GB) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mac M1 Pro** (104.3 GB/s, roofline 206 / 23.9 tok/s) | 97.0 tok/s = **47%** | **12.76 tok/s = 53%** |\n| **NUC i5-1250P** (41.8 GB/s, roofline 82.6 / 9.57 tok/s) | 42.1 tok/s = **51%** | 7.26 tok/s = **76%** |\n\n- **Small models are NOT bandwidth-bound on either host (~50%)** — per-token\n  fixed cost (sampling, detokenise, norms, attention) dominates, so kernel and\n  overhead work still pays there.\n- **Only 7B-on-NUC is near the wall (76%).** \"Kernel micro-optimisation is\n  done\" is TRUE for that one cell and was over-generalised.\n- Mac M1 Pro has **2.5x the NUC's bandwidth** — its 7B roofline is 23.9 tok/s,\n  so compute levers have far more headroom there. Measure per (host, model).\n\nI wrote \"the 7B doesn't fit on the Mac\". WRONG, and user-corrected: 4.37 GB in\na 16 GB UMA box with 159 GB free disk. What was actually measured was a\ntransient: free=0.2 GB, compressed=6.8 GB, swap used 7.8 GB. Under that,\nloading faults pages in one at a time — **RSS crawled to 3.44 GB, still inside\n`[load]` at 290 s, 153M pageins, \u003c100% CPU (one thread blocked on faults, not\n8 workers computing)**. The NUC did the same load+warm in 21 s.\n**Diagnostic tells that a run is fault-bound, not compute-bound: RSS far below\nthe model size, CPU \u003c100% when it should be ~800%, and time stuck in `[load]`.**\nTo test the 7B on the Mac, free memory first (close the browser/editor) and\nre-check `vm_stat` free+inactive \u003e= ~6 GB.\n\nClosing the browser took reclaimable to 4.5 GB. Then: **warm 5.2-8.5 s (was\n430 s), RSS 4.25 GB resident, CPU 318-\u003e680%, 12.76 tok/s median** (runs 8.11 /\n12.76 / 12.89 — the first is the cold-cache outlier).\n\n| host | tok/s | GB/s used | bandwidth | % of roofline |\n|------|-------|-----------|-----------|---------------|\n| **Mac M1 Pro** | **12.76** | 55.7 | 104.3 | **53%** |\n| NUC i5-1250P | 7.26 | 31.7 | 41.8 | **76%** |\n\n**ANSWER TO \"is it memory-bound on Mac too?\": NO.** The Mac is at 53% with\n**11.1 tok/s of headroom**; it is 1.76x the NUC on throughput while having 2.5x\nthe bandwidth — the shortfall IS the compute bound. So:\n- **Kernel/compute work still pays on Apple silicon** (up to ~1.9x before the\n  wall). My \"kernel micro-optimisation is DONE\" was true only for 7B-on-NUC.\n- Bandwidth levers (smaller quant, batching) are the NUC's path; compute levers\n  are the Mac's.\n- **Never generalise a roofline across hosts.** One measurement per (host,\n  model). The regime, not the tok/s, decides which levers exist.\n\nTried to re-measure the Q6_K fold on the M1 Pro (Qwen Q6_K, ~100% Q6_K weights,\nso the change shows undiluted — the right instrument, unlike Mistral at 15%).\n4 interleaved pairs, fresh cache + pkill each run:\n\n    FOLD  median 88.42  range [84.1, 95.1]   spread 11.0\n    BASE  median 80.14  range [62.7, 100.3]  spread 37.6  (= 60% of its median)\n    -\u003e +10.3% median, but only 2/4 pairwise wins\n\n**Unusable.** BASE's own spread is 3x any plausible effect. Compare the NUC on\nthe same change: spreads ~0.4 tok/s, 3/3 pairwise wins, +2.4%. So:\n- **Quote NUC numbers for A/B; quote Mac numbers only for ROOFLINE/regime**\n  (medians of clean isolated runs, where a 2x gap survives the noise).\n- The Mistral arm of the same A/B THRASHED partway through — reclaimable fell\n  4.5 -\u003e 3.7 GB mid-run and the tells appeared (cpu 134%, RSS 3.0 GB \u003c 4.37 GB\n  model). Watch reclaimable BEFORE and DURING, not just before.\n- To make the Mac measurable: free to reclaimable \u003e= 6 GB AND quiesce the\n  desktop session. Otherwise a few-percent kernel effect is unrecoverable.\n\n**The roofline conclusion is unaffected** — it rests on 12.76 vs 7.26 tok/s\nmedians and the corrected bandwidth bench, a ~2x gap, not on a few percent.\n\nThe batch-4 tile called `q4DotMA4` 4x with the SAME block offset: each\n256-weight super-block re-derived its header 4x (4 u32 loads, 2 branchy\nf16ToF32, ~24 shift/and for eight 6-bit scales + eight 6-bit mins). Only the\nACTIVATION offset varies across a tile — the header belongs to the WEIGHT\nblock. **LLVM cannot CSE it**: `Ptr.fromRaw` int-\u003eptr casts kill the alias\nanalysis it would need.\n\nFix = split into header-decode + `q4DotBody(decoded values)`; `q4DotMA4`\ndelegates, so the dot math has ONE definition (the drift failure mode already\ndocumented on `q8RowDot`).\n\n**NUC, Mistral-7B, ~110-tok prompt, 4 interleaved pairs: TTFT 17.24 -\u003e 15.06 s,\n-12.6%, 4/4 pairwise, effect 2.18 s vs BASE spread 0.99 s = SEPARABLE.**\nBit-identical output. **Decode (batch=1) is unaffected BY CONSTRUCTION** — it\nalready decodes once per block. This becomes a DECODE lever only when\nspeculative/batched verification makes decode batch\u003e1.\n\nEverything before it attacked the dot (already optimal: 192 vpdpbusd, VNNI\nfiring) or the dispatch (pool already ~89% utilised). This attacked\nREDUNDANT WORK — the same bytes decoded N times. Look for repeated derivation\nof block-invariant data across a tile before touching kernel arithmetic again.\n\n`q6DotMA4` is called 4x per tile with the same block too, and it re-loads\n`dVec` + re-decodes `d` each time. Q6_K is 15% of Mistral's calls, so the\nprefill win there is smaller but the change is mechanical once q4's split is\nthe template.\n\nThe Q6_K redundancy was BIGGER than Q4_K's: the batch-4 tile re-derived the\nblock scale, 12 weight SIMD loads, 16 sub-block scales and the entire 6-bit\nnibble reconstruction (~10 SIMD ops x 8 sub-blocks) once PER ROW — ~70% of\nper-row work repeated 4x, vs Q4's ~24 shift/and + 2 f16 decodes.\n\nFix is a loop INVERSION, not a hoist: rebuild qLo/qHi once per sub-block and\nrun 4 rows inside, so 2 vectors stay live instead of 16 (all 16 spills — the\ntile-widening 4-\u003e8 failure). 4 results return via a per-band 32-byte scratch\n(Haxe has no out-params; a struct return would allocate per block).\n\nNUC i5-1250P, Mistral-7B, ~110-tok prompt, 4 interleaved pairs, fresh cache:\n  unpinned  BASE median 13.21  HOIST 11.11  **-15.9%**\n  P-pinned  BASE median 24.16  HOIST 21.05  **-12.8%**\n**8/8 pairwise, non-overlapping in both regimes.** Bit-identical on BOTH ISAs\n(aarch64 SDOT and x86_64 VNNI/vpdpbusd), 128-tok Q6_K generation.\n\nFirst A/B was on the Mac with Qwen Q6_K: 2/4 pairwise, effect 0.43s vs spread\n0.95s. The census said why: **Qwen Q6_K issues 2160 Q6_K matmuls vs 13050\nQ8_0** — Q6 is ~14% of CALLS. Mistral is the same ratio by count (2970 vs\n17280) BUT its Q6_K tensor is FFN-DOWN (14336x4096) and FFN is 73.8% of the\nwork, so Q6's WORK share is far above its CALL share. **Call count != work\nshare — weight a census by tensor size before picking a benchmark model.**\nUse `NUE_DUMP_Q4_GATES=1` BEFORE choosing the vehicle, not after a null.\n\nUnpinned BASE spread was ~14% of median (one 14.85s outlier among ~13s runs):\nthreads migrating between P- and E-cores, so each run samples a different\nmachine. But pinning is the WRONG fix here: confining to 4 P-cores drops the 8\nE-cores, costing **~1.8x throughput (24.2s vs 13.2s) AND widening spread to\n5.5s**. It is a latency knob. **For a throughput-parallel kernel, run\nunpinned and use interleaved pairs + non-overlap, not pinning, to control\nhybrid-scheduler variance.** (Mac contrast: UMA, so CPU/GPU share bandwidth\nand memory PRESSURE, not core type, is what perturbs it.)\n\n\nApplying the q6 inversion to `q4DotBody` looked like the biggest remaining\nprefill lever: `subDotVec` loads+unpacks weights PER ROW, and each 32-byte\ngroup is read TWICE per row (low nibbles, then high), so a batch-4 tile does\n64 weight loads + 64 nibble extracts where 8 + 16 suffice. Q4_K_M is 85% of\nMistral's matmuls (17280 vs 2970) vs Q6's 15% — 6x the call share of the win\nthat had just landed.\n\n**It measured SLOWER. NUC, Mistral-7B, ~110-tok, 4 interleaved pairs, TTFT:**\n    BASE median 10.95   INV median 12.52   **+14.3%, 0/4 pairwise, non-overlapping**\nBit-identical on ARM (verified before measuring), so this was purely a cost\nquestion. REVERTED.\n\nq6's kernel already had ONE isum accumulator per row and did its nibble\nreconstruction in a straight line, so inverting only moved work out.\n\nq4's `subDotVec` CHAINS its accumulator: `acc = dotI8I7(acc, a, w)` across the\ntwo halves. Inverting broke the chain — each dot became\n`dotI8I7(SIMD4i32.splat(0), ...)`, so I added **64 zero-vector materialisations\nand 32 extra vector multiplies per super-block** (the `sc*(h0+h1)` -\u003e\n`sc*h0 + sc*h1` distribution doubles the multiplies) to save 56 loads and 48\nnibble ops. Plus 4 isum + 2 weight + 2 scale vectors live at once, which does\nnot fit x86's 16 xmm.\n\n**RULE: before inverting a loop to share loads, check whether the inner op is\nan ACCUMULATOR CHAIN. Breaking a chain costs a zero-init and an extra add per\nterm, and that can exceed the loads saved.** Loads are cheap when L1-resident\n(a row's weights are ~2.3 KB); dependency structure is not.\n\nThird instance of the same lesson: tile widening 4-\u003e8 (+5.9%), this (+14.3%),\nvs the two that worked (q4 header hoist -12.6%, q6 inversion -15.9%). Redundant\nWORK is a real lever; redundant LOADS in L1 often are not.\n\nThe FUSED multi-weight band (second batch-4 tile) still calls `q4DotMA4`, so it\nnever received the e6a66b75 header hoist at all. That hoist is proven (-12.6%)\nand is separable from the inversion — worth applying there alone, after\nchecking the fused path is actually exercised.\n\n`[q4-gate] fused_matmul=off env=\u003cunset\u003e`. `useFusedMatmul()` is opt-in\n(`NUE_FUSED_MATMUL`) and its own comment records the verdict: full llama-chat\nruns on macOS REGRESSED vs the split kernels. So the second batch-4 tile does\nnot execute by default, and hoisting its header would optimise nothing.\n\n**Correction to the q6 result (2913bdc1):** that commit edited BOTH batch-4\ntiles, and the second one is this dead fused band. The measured -15.9% is\ntherefore attributable to the FIRST (live) tile alone — the change is still\ncorrect and the number still stands, but half the diff is in a path that does\nnot run. Its per-band 32-byte scratch is likewise allocated but unused; left\nin place so the two tiles stay consistent if the fused path is ever revived.\n\n**Check `[q4-gate]`/`[q4-census]` BEFORE optimising a code path**, the same way\nvehicle selection needs the census first. Both are one run with\n`NUE_DUMP_Q4_GATES=1`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_attention_split_measured_ffn_is_the_lever` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}